    "examples": [
      {
        "question_text": "Determine whether each expression is a polynomial. If so, name it by its degree where possible.\n(a) $-\\dfrac{5}{7}x^{4}+13x-17$\n(b) $8x^{2}+\\sqrt{x}-19$\n(c) $x^{3}+\\dfrac{11}{x}+23$\n(d) $7^{x}+x^{2}$\n(e) $\\sqrt{11}\\,x^{2}-3x+29$\n(f) $31$",
        "solution_text": "(a) All the indices of $x$ are whole numbers. The highest is $4$.\nQuartic polynomial. The coefficient $-\\dfrac{5}{7}$ is allowed.\n(b) $\\sqrt{x}=x^{\\frac{1}{2}}$ has a fractional index.\nNot a polynomial.\n(c) $\\dfrac{11}{x}=11x^{-1}$ has a negative index.\nNot a polynomial.\n(d) In $7^{x}$, $x$ is in the index.\nNot a polynomial.\n(e) The root is on the coefficient, not on $x$. The indices of $x$ are $2$ and $1$.\nQuadratic polynomial.\n(f) $31=31x^{0}$.\nConstant polynomial, degree $0$."
      },
      {
        "question_text": "For each polynomial, state its degree, leading term, leading coefficient and constant term, and say whether it is monic or non-monic. Also state the coefficient of $x^{2}$.\n(a) $17+13x-5x^{6}$\n(b) $x^{4}-7x^{2}+19x$",
        "solution_text": "(a) The term with the highest index is $-5x^{6}$, even though it is written last.\nDegree: $6$\nLeading term: $-5x^{6}$\nLeading coefficient: $-5$\nConstant term: $17$\nNon-monic.\nThere is no $x^{2}$ term, so its coefficient is $0$.\n(b) Degree: $4$\nLeading term: $x^{4}$\nLeading coefficient: $1$\nConstant term: $0$\nMonic.\nThe coefficient of $x^{2}$ is $-7$."
      },
      {
        "question_text": "State the degree, leading term and leading coefficient of each polynomial.\n(a) $7x(x+5)(x-11)$\n(b) $\\dfrac{13x^{2}-17x^{5}}{19}$",
        "solution_text": "(a) The highest-index term comes from multiplying the $x$-terms.\n$7x\\times x\\times x=7x^{3}$\nDegree: $3$\nLeading term: $7x^{3}$\nLeading coefficient: $7$\n(b) $\\dfrac{13x^{2}-17x^{5}}{19}=\\dfrac{13}{19}x^{2}-\\dfrac{17}{19}x^{5}$\nDegree: $5$\nLeading term: $-\\dfrac{17}{19}x^{5}$\nLeading coefficient: $-\\dfrac{17}{19}$"
      },
      {
        "question_text": "The polynomial $(a+5)x^{4}+(b-8)x^{3}+c-13$ has degree $3$, is monic and has constant term $19$. Find $a$, $b$ and $c$.",
        "solution_text": "Degree $3$ means the coefficient of $x^{4}$ must be $0$.\n$\\begin{align*}a+5&=0\\\\a&=-5\\end{align*}$\nThe leading coefficient must be $1$ because the polynomial is monic.\n$\\begin{align*}b-8&=1\\\\b&=9\\end{align*}$\nThe constant term is $19$.\n$\\begin{align*}c-13&=19\\\\c&=32\\end{align*}$\nSo the polynomial is $x^{3}+19$."
      }
    ],
